ing $n=-1$ gives $x=-{90}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<0.$ \n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={-180}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={-90}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343066 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343067 13194139535309 true] [17592186343066 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535309 true] [17592186343066 1041 "field-1" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343066 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343067 13194139535309 true] [17592186343066 1110 #uuid "bac141b0-1322-5b9e-bf4e-e58b94ef95c9" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343067 1034 "-180" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343067 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535309 true] [17592186343067 1111 #uuid "1eb68341-b796-5347-a7b8-8e1c6144b5aa" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343068 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343069 13194139535309 true] [17592186343068 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535309 true] [17592186343068 1041 "field-2" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343068 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343069 13194139535309 true] [17592186343068 1110 #uuid "5b3c713c-136a-5aca-ae53-2b5eb3571ad8" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343069 1034 "-90" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343069 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535309 true] [17592186343069 1111 #uuid "d501dd71-637d-5a79-9da9-43a3412b618f" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343070 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343071 13194139535309 true] [17592186343070 1089 "q-82373" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343070 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535309 true] [17592186343070 1100 "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-{45}^{∘})-\\sqrt{3}=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343070 1113 #uuid "ca5d3a4d-d32a-5591-b61b-0726227bd885" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343070 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-{45}^{∘})-\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-{45}^{∘}) & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-{45}^{∘}) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-{45}^{∘}) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-{45}^{∘} & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={45}^{∘}+{45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={45}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={135}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={225}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x={315}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={45}^{∘},{135}^{∘},{225}^{∘},{315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is $45\\cdot 135=6\\,075.$" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343072 13194139535309 true] [17592186343071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343073 13194139535309 true] [17592186343071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343074 13194139535309 true] [17592186343071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343075 13194139535309 true] [17592186343071 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343076 13194139535309 true] [17592186343071 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535309 true] [17592186343071 1041 "selection" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343071 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343076 13194139535309 true] [17592186343071 1110 #uuid "5157c32d-d421-56a1-994d-8a3d81fedcc9" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343072 1034 "3\\,265" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343072 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535309 true] [17592186343072 1111 #uuid "803984a4-031c-5a40-820c-8fa963cf49f3" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343073 1034 "6\\,750" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343073 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535309 true] [17592186343073 1111 #uuid "fb1150d8-fbf5-54ac-8f7c-5d00aa29736a" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343074 1034 "3\\,005" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343074 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535309 true] [17592186343074 1111 #uuid "f01c2a25-6356-5cf6-8c46-114c3db75ddc" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343075 1034 "3\\,526" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343075 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535309 true] [17592186343075 1111 #uuid "d5f61731-1626-5143-9f3b-1411cc5557f8" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343076 1034 "6\\,075" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343076 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535309 true] [17592186343076 1111 #uuid "bf8d4ab6-6044-5b97-af0d-ad2e27cff690" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343077 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343078 13194139535309 true] [17592186343077 1089 "q-8479" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343077 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535309 true] [17592186343077 1100 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ Given that the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343077 1113 #uuid "c4570ce6-8681-560b-93a0-ff873c068a50" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343077 1145 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{12}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\pi }{2}+\\frac{5\\pi }{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{24}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{24}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{29\\pi }{24}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{41\\pi }{24}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5\\pi }{24},\\frac{17\\pi }{24},\\frac{29\\pi }{24},\\frac{41\\pi }{24}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=\\frac{5\\pi }{24}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{17\\pi }{24},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{24}\\cdot \\frac{17\\pi }{24}=\\frac{85{\\pi }^{2}}{576}.\n$$" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343079 13194139535309 true] [17592186343078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343080 13194139535309 true] [17592186343078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343081 13194139535309 true] [17592186343078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343082 13194139535309 true] [17592186343078 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343083 13194139535309 true] [17592186343078 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535309 true] [17592186343078 1041 "selection" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343078 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343082 13194139535309 true] [17592186343078 1110 #uuid "e2a71cf1-f245-5ce1-94e5-a1187e76e035" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343079 1034 "\\frac{85{\\pi }^{2}}{24}" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343079 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535309 true] [17592186343079 1111 #uuid "a7508151-97d1-52bb-bac2-9bad8852abcb" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343080 1034 "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{24}" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343080 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535309 true] [17592186343080 1111 #uuid "33bd5351-db78-5a1d-94ec-cfeffa31ba3b" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343081 1034 "\\frac{85\\pi }{576}" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343081 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535309 true] [17592186343081 1111 #uuid "4df24be7-7040-5495-b853-b92e41afb9e9" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343082 1034 "\\frac{85{\\pi }^{2}}{576}" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343082 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535309 true] [17592186343082 1111 #uuid "21693bd1-8502-56ad-a254-0db26b8ff31f" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343083 1034 "\\frac{17{\\pi }^{2}}{576}" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343083 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535309 true] [17592186343083 1111 #uuid "9681b628-9399-5a9f-8b7f-8b6a234949fc" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343084 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343085 13194139535309 true] [17592186343084 1089 "q-8557" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343084 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535309 true] [17592186343084 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=1$ for $0\\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343084 1113 #uuid "3eb110db-f4a7-568d-994f-26f7cca1ce4e" 13194139535309 true] [17592186343084 1145 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{11\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{12}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{24}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(12n+25)\\pi }{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=-2$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{24}$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{24}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{24},\\frac{13\\pi }{24}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{24}\\cdot \\frac{13\\pi }{24}=\\frac{13{\\pi }^{2}}{576}.\n$$" 13194139535